<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanzimat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tanzimat was a period of reform in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1839 to 1876. Prompted by growing European military and economic superiority, it sought to modernise administration, law, taxation and the army, and proclaimed the equality of Ottoman subjects regardless of religion. Accounts of the Ottoman Empire present it as a major attempt to strengthen the state from within, culminating in the first Ottoman constitution of 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanzimat.jpg" alt="Tanzimat" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juris Tiltins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Ottoman_Empire_(1882%E2%80%931922).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Through the 1700s structural problems in Ottoman governance led to defeats in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1686-1700">Russo-Turkish Wars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a>, and showed that the empire could not compete with European armies. Provincial governors, the ayans, and local leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a> increasingly defied the centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a>, a reform-minded sultan, waited until the Auspicious Incident of 1826 to destroy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, and after that his reign was a period of Western reform and centralisation. In 1838 the Treaty of Balta Liman with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> dismantled Ottoman trade monopolies.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> died suddenly on 1 July 1839 and was succeeded by his son Abdul Mejid I. Reformists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-resid-pasha">Mustafa Reşid Pasha</a>, who had been ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, argued that European institutions could restore imperial power. Their ideas took shape in the Gülhane Edict, issued on 3 November 1839.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The edict promised security of life, property and honour for all subjects, fair taxation and conscription, public trials and the end of tax farming, the iltizam. It guaranteed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a> to non-Muslims as well and put an end to the kul system, under which a ruler's servants could be executed or lose their property at his will. Statutes followed that created new ministries, the first paper banknotes in 1840, a regular method of army recruitment in 1843-44 and the first nationwide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> in 1844. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">Telegraph</a> networks began in 1847 and railways in 1856.</p><p>The Reform Edict of 1856 promised full legal equality to citizens of all religions and abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, though it was replaced with an exemption payment for military service. Secular Nizamiye Courts were set up in 1864, a Nationality Law of 1869 created a common Ottoman citizenship, and the Mecelle, a civil code, was published between 1869 and 1876. The reforms peaked in 1876 with an Ottoman constitution, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midhat-pasha">Midhat Pasha</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The reforms met resistance from conservative clerics who feared secular courts and schools, from officials who defended their privileges, and from Muslim artisans and peasants who lost tax exemptions and faced European competition. Christians in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> often wanted autonomy that was harder to obtain under a centralised state, and the 1875 Herzegovina Uprising was partly fuelled by tax grievances. Foreign borrowing led to bankruptcy in 1875, and in 1881 European powers set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-public-debt-administration">Ottoman Public Debt Administration</a> to control revenues.</p><p>Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a> signed the 1876 constitution but soon turned against it. The period is held to have ended with the Great Eastern Crisis of 1875-1878, although reform continued. Historians disagree about its effect: some credit it with building a powerful national government, while others argue that it speeded the empire's fragmentation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tanzimat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Tanzimat</b> reforms of 1839–1876, prompted by growing European military and economic superiority, sought to modernise administration and law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its users called it lisân-ı Türkî or zebân-ı Türkî, “the Turkish language”, or lisân-ı Rûm, “the language of Rum”, and more generally Türkçe or Türkî, “Turkish”; only in the <b>Tanzimat</b> era (1839–1876) did the name “Ottoman”, lisân-ı Osmânî or Osmanlıca, come into use.</blockquote><a href="/language/ottoman-turkish">Ottoman Turkish · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Ottoman Empire the <b>Tanzimat</b> reforms, from 1839, promised equality to subjects of every religion.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The CUP’s drive for centralization continued an effort begun with the <b>Tanzimat</b> reforms.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Attempts to stave off the empire’s collapse began chiefly with the 19th-century <b>Tanzimat</b> reforms; the Young Ottomans of the mid-century tried to build an Ottoman nationalism, with limited democracy, against rising ethnic nationalism, and after their demise under Sultan Abdul Hamid II’s absolutist reign the Young Turks took…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/kemalism">Kemalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Ottoman Empire and the Middle East liberalism influenced the <b>Tanzimat</b> and Al-Nahda reforms, the Young Ottomans and Islamic Modernism, though its books and papers reached mainly intellectuals and the emerging middle class.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
